module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_field_default_is_evaluated_in_the_callers_scope

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data a_field_default_is_evaluated_in_the_callers_scope: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_field_default_is_evaluated_in_the_callers_scope" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundBelowTheLadder, ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "resolving a declared default's reads at the point of DECLARATION -- a carrier that records, for each declared default, the referent each of its bare reads means in the declaring module, so neither the interpreter NOR the type layer consults an environment for them. Sufficient for: no call site, and no size or shape of a default expression, changes which declaration a name inside a default means. Two of this class's three paths do not have it yet and the class rung is their minimum, stated path by path in the PER-PATH STANDING receipt: the service-operation input-block route evaluates a declaring-site default now but the value it bound is unobservable at the refusal, because v1_interpreter.rs `rest_exchange_selection` drops the invocation that `RestReplayExchangeAbsent` carries, and v1_compiler_infer.rs `parameter_default_emissions` still types an omitted argument's default in the CALL SITE's InferScope, which the executed base leg proved SILENT -- the witness module loaded clean and the refusal came at runtime." } },

  receipts: [
    "a field default is evaluated in the CALLER'S environment, not the declaring module's. A field whose default expression references a constructor (or any name) bound only in the declaring module fails with NoSuchVariable at every call site that omits that field. The caller sees no diagnostic connecting the failure to the default; the variable name is all the error carries. The declaring module compiled clean, the operation declaration typechecked, and the failure is invisible until the operation is CALLED without that argument.",

    "SPECIMEN, found 2026-11-09 (heal-publish run 42677477262, PR #16176, regression from #16160). dag/extdeps/cloud/gcp/sts.dag declares `subject_token_type: SubjectTokenType = Jwt` and `requested_token_type: RequestedTokenType = RequestAccessToken` in the Exchange operation's input block. Jwt and RequestAccessToken are nullary constructors of types declared in the same sts.dag module. dag/gunbc/heal_publication.dag imports the module as a whole (`import extdeps.cloud.gcp.sts`) without a named-import list, so Jwt is not bound in heal_publication's scope -- though the executed pair later showed the named-import list would not have saved it either: a witness caller that names its arms in its import list fails at base exactly as one that names none. When heal_publication_publish_wet calls gcp.STS.Exchange without subject_token_type, the interpreter's call_function_inner evaluates the default expression `Jwt` via eval_expr(default_node, env, ctx) where env is the caller's environment, and raises NoSuchVariable\{Jwt}. Every heal-publish candidate on every PR fails.",

    "THE LANGUAGE-LAYER DEFECT: this is a scoping violation at the evaluator level. Field defaults are part of the operation's declaration and their names should resolve in the declaring module's scope. The evaluator instead threads the caller's environment, coupling every operation's defaults to what its callers happen to import. The consequence is a fragility class: any default expression that references an unexported or differently-scoped name fails at every call site that omits the field, with a misleading NoSuchVariable naming a constructor the caller has never seen.",

    "THE INTERIM REMEDY (hotfix #16185, e4112bea): import the constructors by name at the caller's module AND pass them explicitly at the call site, so the default is never evaluated. This is per-call-site, does not fix the class, and leaves every other default built from an unimported constructor latent for the next caller to trip over. #16185's explicit fields at the `heal_publication` call site are STILL IN PLACE after the language fix (gunb-ai/gunbc#16196), deliberately: the executed witness covers a plain function parameter's default, and that call goes through the service-operation route with a host boundary, which the witness does not cover. Removing them is a follow-up for an operation-input-block witness, not a deletion this row will vouch for. THE HOTFIX'S FIRST HALF NEVER WORKED, AND THE PAIRED RUN SHOWS IT: #16185 did two things -- it named `Jwt` and `RequestAccessToken` in the caller's import list AND passed them explicitly at the call site. If a caller's own named import put an arm where a default could read it, the import half alone would have sufficed and the explicit arguments would be dead weight. They are not dead weight: a witness caller that DOES name its arms in its import list fails at base with the same `undefined variable` refusal as a caller that names none (the `importing_caller_omitted_argument_still_binds` cell, verbatim above). So the defect never depended on the caller's import list -- which is the difference between a scoping bug in the evaluator and a caller that forgot to import something, and the reason the hotfix could only ever be per-call-site.",

    "THE FIX (gunb-ai/gunbc#16196, head c0ae1a1796dcab2b39d0ac891ba8ed89057b0e00). `call_function_inner` and the service-operation sibling `build_service_param_env` now evaluate an omitted argument's default through `declaring_scope_env`: the run's entry environment (the caller's frames are dropped -- a caller's local is not part of what a declaration means), with the fieldless arm the DECLARING SITE CAN NAME bound over it by spelling. Which arm that is comes from the authority the ambiguous-read census already uses -- `site_resolved_variant_arm_module`, reached through the one-line delegation `InterpContext::declaring_module_for_arm_read`: the declaring file's own coproduct, or the one its own file imports that name from. Not a second rule: this file already carries the record of what two copies of one rule do, its own note on `site_resolved_fn` that an earlier revision left the interpreter its own copy and the two copies had already disagreed. Fail-closed the same way as before: a module that merely sits in the scope answers nothing, two visible coproducts spelling one arm answer nothing, a natively-realized arm keeps its native answer, and nothing reaches the corpus pool -- a name the declaring site itself cannot name still raises NoSuchVariable, which is what the unimported-bare-name floor (v1_interpreter.rs `unimported_bare_name_floor_test`) already demands of a bare name. There is no cap and no fallback path in the walk that finds the reads -- a walk that stopped early would hand a name back to the caller's scope, which is this class re-entering through the size of a default instead of through its call site (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed; review 92681 on #16196 caught the first head doing exactly that and the cap was deleted, not documented).",

    "EXECUTED RECEIPT, PAIRED BASE VERSUS HEAD, run by the remote build owner (cited by the verifier, not by this row's author). Base leg base/field-default-witness at 02d33efa10aee50d3df1a8153d337a817702abb8 -- origin/main e4112beac6f plus the four dag/test/claim files and NOTHING else, no interpreter change; head leg c0ae1a1796dcab2b39d0ac891ba8ed89057b0e00. Both built in one cargo build --release -p v1-compiler --bin gunbc --bin claim_executor --bin claim_batch, rc=0, 382 s at base and 403 s at head, on fresh runners (BuildBuddy invocations 240466d2-a0b4-40b5-92d5-7ff449bad304 and 6b649f7c-c0c2-4001-b09f-ca503c72f4d6), gunbc sha256 6f390f8836c7775fd5f28cc46c367f6d33f79b1872ac9b1fcefc71661ced207c at base and 8c5834e7d20fc6d413ab17085ffa576802a2fcf8720e1950416aeec1d3c94589 at head. The witness set runs with --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 and no --hermetic, the same invocation the CI witness path uses.",

    "THE THREE WITNESS CELLS, VERBATIM. BASE rc=1: FAIL field_default_omitted_argument_resolves_in_the_declaring_module (runtime error [no-such-variable]: undefined variable: FieldDefaultBearer); FAIL second_field_default_omitted_argument_resolves_in_the_declaring_module (runtime error [no-such-variable]: undefined variable: FieldDefaultOpaque); FAIL imported_arm_field_default_omitted_argument_resolves_in_the_declaring_module (runtime error [no-such-variable]: undefined variable: FieldDefaultCarrierAck). HEAD rc=0: the same three names PASS. The refusals are RUNTIME, after a clean load -- claim_batch logs the resolve of the witness module and the cell count, so these are not shape or resolve refusals dressed up as behaviour. That is the specimen reproduced in miniature: a runtime error naming a constructor the caller has never seen, from a declaration that compiled clean.",

    "THE FOURTH DISCRIMINATING CELL, WHICH WAS DESIGNED AS A CONTROL AND REFUSED TO BE ONE. The cell `importing_caller_omitted_argument_still_binds` is a caller that DOES name the arms in its import list -- #16185's shape. It was expected green at base. It is RED at base, rc=1, with the identical refusal: FAIL importing_caller_omitted_argument_still_binds (runtime error [no-such-variable]: undefined variable: FieldDefaultBearer); green at head, rc=0. Two things follow, and the row records both rather than smoothing them. First, the witness set still isolates the caller scope, but not the way it was designed to: what separates the cells is not whether the caller imported the arm -- at base nothing a caller imports reaches a default -- it is whether the DECLARING site can name it. Second, the supplied-argument cell `field_default_supplied_argument_binds_as_declared` is the remaining green-on-both-sides anchor (rc=0 at base and at head), so the set is not passing on a general failure of the fixture.",

    "THE MODULE THAT BROKE heal-publish STILL LOADS, PAIRED. `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/heal_publication.dag --function heal_publisher_lifetime_ceiling_refusal` (a pure nullary entry: no net, no clock) returns rc=2 on BOTH legs with the identical text, error: function `heal_publisher_lifetime_ceiling_refusal` returned `Optional`, not `ProcessExit` -- the entry contract refusing the value type, after the module resolved and the function evaluated, with no undefined-variable diagnostic anywhere. Both legs rc=2 with the same rendering is the comparison grain that entry allows; `gunbc run` prints an Optional result as just `Optional`.",

    "WHAT THE PAIRED RUN DOES NOT COVER, STATED AS A GAP. The corpus's own arm defaults (`attempt_filter: WorkflowJobAttemptFilter = AttemptsLatest` and the `= Comment` / `= ListStateOpen` family in `dag/extdeps/github/`) were meant to be covered by `dag/test/claim/github_workflow_runs_witness.dag`, 27 cells. UNOBSERVED ON BOTH LEGS: it refuses at load identically at base and head, 0 of 27 run, every row closing with `dag/test/claim/github_workflow_runs_witness.dag:4:29: error: unresolved type 'LiveTreeDisposition'` plus `undefined variable 'SubstrateInputsOnly'` and the WorkflowRunStatus/Conclusion arms at :17, :34, :38, :42, :46, :50, :54, :93, :97, :101, :105, :109. That is import drift already on main, unrelated to this change and identical on both legs, so the run gives ZERO evidence about whether relocating the default moved those defaults -- it is not green, it was not run, and it is named here as the open gap it is rather than dropped from the record. What was substituted, in two grades of evidence: RESOLUTION of the three files that own those defaults (dag/extdeps/github/workflow_runs.dag, dag/extdeps/github/pulls.dag, dag/extdeps/cloud/gcp/sts.dag) is IDENTICAL base versus head and clean on both, driven by a scratch probe fn appended in the run tree, rc=2 in 7-8 s each, printing the entry-contract refusal 'error: function `qn_load_probe` returned `true`, not `ProcessExit`', per-file diff between legs empty; invocations 3fbd3c06-c24e-4044-b979-d4396fe3c57d and 99f98a8b-a0dd-42d6-ad4f-9b89ca286520) -- which says resolution moved, nothing about values; and one EVALUATION of such a default on the operation route, in the PATH 2 receipt below, which discriminates but stops at the binding step.",


    "PER-PATH STANDING (gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder: a class's rung is the MINIMUM across its in-scope paths at a declared grain, so this row reports its paths one by one instead of letting its best path speak for the class). PATH 1, THE INTERPRETER'S PLAIN-PARAMETER ROUTE (v1_interpreter.rs call_function_inner): CLOSED AND CLAIMED. Four cells refuse at base with the reported runtime [no-such-variable] and pass at head -- three non-importing callers and, unexpectedly, a caller that DOES name the arms in its import list (#16185's shape, which the paired run showed was never fixed by importing anything) -- with one supplied-argument cell green on BOTH sides as the anchor that the set is not passing on a general refusal; a revert of the rule goes red there rather than being caught by a reader. Read on its own that path is mechanically preventable, WITH ITS LIMIT NAMED because an unenrolled claim is mitigation wearing a claim's clothes: its force depends on the claim corpus under --source-root dag executing and staying enrolled, and this row's author did not establish that a merge-BLOCKING lane runs it, as distinct from the heal and convergence paths that demonstrably do. PATH 2, THE SERVICE-OPERATION INPUT-BLOCK ROUTE (build_service_param_env -- the route the Exchange specimen actually takes): OBSERVED, AND IT DISCRIMINATES. A scratch probe module in the verifier's run tree (blob 1c5d9551, identical on both legs, nothing committed) evaluates a zero-argument call to `github.Pulls.List(auth_token, owner, repo)` -- no `state`, no `per_page` -- inside a witness replay frame holding one Read grant, ServiceOpTree { service: "github.Pulls" } and a replay fixture store with ZERO fixtures, driven by `gunbc run --entry dag/test/claim/qn_pulls_default_probe.dag --function qn_pulls_default_probe`. BASE rc=2, 10 s, https://app.buildbuddy.io/invocation/223e292b-60da-40e2-8728-fe08ed84a87d: error: function `qn_pulls_default_probe` returned `undefined variable: ListStateOpen`, not `ProcessExit` -- the reported defect, on the corpus's own operation-input-block default. HEAD rc=2, 9 s, https://app.buildbuddy.io/invocation/15238095-dac9-432f-88cf-8531bac9d05f: error: function `qn_pulls_default_probe` returned `type error: missing exact REST replay fixture for bound invocation; real transport refused`, not `ProcessExit` -- the default evaluated, the invocation bound, and the frame refusing at the NEXT step because no fixture exists, which is what zero fixtures is designed to do. WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH, AND WHY THE HOTFIX STAYS: it shows the omitted `state` no longer raises undefined-variable; it does NOT show which arm `state` took, because with no fixture nothing prints the bound request. So #16185's explicit `subject_token_type: Jwt` / `requested_token_type: RequestAccessToken` at the heal_publication call site are NOT deleted by this diff: removing them would put the first production traffic on a route where the evaluation step is observed and the VALUE is not. The capability that closes this path is NOT a hand-written replay fixture, and that was checked rather than assumed: matching a REST replay is EXACT on five fields (dag/extdeps/transports/rest.dag `rest_bound_invocation_eq`: at, method, target, input_digest, auth_identity), and two of them are computed host-side and never surfaced to .dag code -- `rest_bound_invocation_value` in v1_interpreter.rs builds `input_digest` as a structural hash over the BOUND inputs (exactly where `state`'s default lands) and `target` as the realized URL including the query. Keying a fixture by hand would mean inventing that key, and worse, presupposing `state=open`, the very value under test, so a match would prove nothing. THE NAMED BLOCKER, precisely: the bound invocation is computed and is carried to the point of refusal (rest.dag's `RestReplayExchangeAbsent { invocation: RestBoundOperationInvocation }`) and is then DISCARDED -- v1_interpreter.rs `rest_exchange_selection` turns that variant into a bare `TypeError` reading 'missing exact REST replay fixture for bound invocation; real transport refused' and drops the carried invocation. So the one value that would answer the open/closed question exists at runtime and is thrown away at the seam. What unblocks the path is a small interpreter change -- render `invocation.target` into that refusal, or return it as a typed refusal a witness can read -- after which the SAME zero-fixture probe prints the realized URL at head and the question is answered by reading it, no fixture needed. Deliberately NOT taken here: it is a second behaviour change in a PR about where a default is read, and it deserves its own claim. Until it lands, this path is evaluated-but-unvalued and #16185's explicit fields stay. PATH 3, THE TYPE LAYER (v1_compiler_infer.rs parameter_default_emissions): UNCHANGED, AND THE EXECUTED BASE LEG SHOWS IT SILENT -- the witness module LOADED clean (claim_batch logged 'resolved ... running 3 witness(es)') and nothing refused an omitted-argument default typed in the CALL SITE's InferScope; the failure surfaced only at runtime. Silent wrongness is below the ladder and not a rung. THE CLASS RUNG IS THE MINIMUM OF THE THREE: FoundBelowTheLadder. The interpreter fix is what it is -- one of three paths closed, two still able to make one default mean different things at different call sites.",

    "WHAT CLIMBS, AND WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE. Closed: a default reading a fieldless arm of a coproduct the declaring site can NAME -- its own coproduct's arm, the Jwt shape, and an arm it reaches through a named import. Left open, deliberately un-closed by this diff and named so the next reader does not read the fix as wider than it is: a default's reads that are not arms keep whatever answer the entry environment gives them today, because `build_initial_env` evaluates every `DataItem` into the entry environment (which is why `sts.dag`'s `scope: NonEmptyStr = cloud_platform_scope`, a default reading a row from ANOTHER module, was never in this class while `Jwt` was) and a call resolves through `lookup_fn`, which is already anchored to the referring file. A corpus scan of the tree's own bare-identifier defaults says the closed population is the one that was actually exposed: of the 33 defaults that read a bare name, six read `none`, one reads a data row from another module, and the rest -- `ListStateOpen`, `Comment`, `AttemptsLatest`, the fixture's and witness' arms -- name a coproduct declared in the default's own file. None of them needs the caller's scope, which is why none of them was expected to move when the caller frames were dropped -- an expectation the paired run tested rather than assumed, and one it partly falsified: the corpus files that own those defaults have no claim cell that reaches them (`dag/test/claim/github_workflow_runs_witness.dag` does not load on main, 0 of 27 run on either leg), so that population is UNOBSERVED and is named as a gap above rather than counted as green. THE SIBLING QUESTION (DESIGN 6b, root generalization) was asked of v2 and the answer is NO: `v2.compiler.infer` `infer_binder_default_check` judges a binder's default in the DECLARING module's scope, and v2's lowering leaves the default on its binder rather than substituting it at the call site -- so v2 has neither the interpreter's defect nor a type-layer emission that could inherit one.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "extdeps.cloud.gcp.sts", decl_name: "Exchange", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.field_default_declaring_scope_fixture", decl_name: "field_default_is_bearer", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.field_default_declaring_scope_witness", decl_name: "field_default_omitted_argument_resolves_in_the_declaring_module", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.field_default_declaring_scope_witness", decl_name: "imported_arm_field_default_omitted_argument_resolves_in_the_declaring_module", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.field_default_importing_caller_control", decl_name: "importing_caller_omitted_argument_still_binds", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
